  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\qt0066_5b74137e174d.png\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 190px;\"/></p><p>As the voltmeter is idea, no current will flow through it. Therefore, considering left loop, we can write</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.2</mo></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p><p>Now, potential drop across the cell which is connected in parallel with Voltmeter,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>25</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p>"
